\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Charging residual RMS and $I_{\rm  fund}$ threshold by method (log scale). Residual floor is the same for pre-fault and voltage methods ($\approx 0.001\,\text  {pu}$), but the voltage method enables a lower threshold (0.08\,pu vs.\ 0.12\,pu) because it does not require a pre-fault window and therefore has a tighter known security margin. Total threshold reduction from TR-11 to TR-15: 60\% (0.20 $\to $ 0.08\,pu).}}{6}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:residual}{{3}{6}{Charging residual RMS and $I_{\rm fund}$ threshold by method (log scale). Residual floor is the same for pre-fault and voltage methods ($\approx 0.001\pu $), but the voltage method enables a lower threshold (0.08\,pu vs.\ 0.12\,pu) because it does not require a pre-fault window and therefore has a tighter known security margin. Total threshold reduction from TR-11 to TR-15: 60\% (0.20 $\to $ 0.08\,pu)}{figure.caption.6}{}}
